{
  "source": "Prompts/critical-thinking-reroll-4.mp4",
  "duration": 196.8355625,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 4.38,
      "text": "This graphic lays out a basic equation for how we process information."
    },
    {
      "start": 5.22,
      "end": 11.22,
      "text": "Having knowledge is essential, but what you choose to do with that knowledge is what actually dictates your results."
    },
    {
      "start": 12.16,
      "end": 14.84,
      "text": "Look at the left side. Knowledge forms your baseline."
    },
    {
      "start": 15.64,
      "end": 21.14,
      "text": "It helps you understand a subject and recognize when something in a text or a prompt doesn't add up."
    },
    {
      "start": 21.68,
      "end": 23.48,
      "text": "On the right side is the active component."
    },
    {
      "start": 23.48,
      "end": 32.0,
      "text": "Critical thinking means you question a claim, examine the evidence, and consider alternative explanations before you decide what to do next."
    },
    {
      "start": 32.84,
      "end": 37.58,
      "text": "When you combine these two halves, the result is better questions and better decisions."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.34,
      "end": 42.28,
      "text": "Be smarter than the tool. It isn't don't trust anything either."
    },
    {
      "start": 42.74,
      "end": 46.72,
      "text": "It's the habit of asking what would have to be true for a claim to hold up."
    },
    {
      "start": 46.72,
      "end": 55.8,
      "text": "In 2015, news outlets around the world published stories about a scientific study claiming a delicious new method for losing weight."
    },
    {
      "start": 56.52,
      "end": 59.22,
      "text": "One front page ran the headline slim by chocolate."
    },
    {
      "start": 60.16,
      "end": 63.06,
      "text": "The face value reaction was immediate. Sounds great."
    },
    {
      "start": 63.86,
      "end": 64.56,
      "text": "I believe it."
    },
    {
      "start": 65.16,
      "end": 67.8,
      "text": "But a critical thinking reaction takes a different path."
    },
    {
      "start": 68.42,
      "end": 70.28,
      "text": "Wait. What's behind the claim?"
    },
    {
      "start": 71.12,
      "end": 75.78,
      "text": "You must force yourself to pause, especially when a claim matches what you want to believe."
    },
    {
      "start": 75.78,
      "end": 82.52,
      "text": "The study itself actually existed. However, it was deliberately designed to be scientifically flimsy."
    },
    {
      "start": 83.32,
      "end": 86.2,
      "text": "This diagram illustrates the primary flaw in the data."
    },
    {
      "start": 86.86,
      "end": 94.82,
      "text": "The researchers used a tiny sample size of only 15 participants, but they tracked 18 entirely different measurements for each person."
    },
    {
      "start": 95.42,
      "end": 100.48,
      "text": "With that many measurements in such a small group, a chance result could look like an important discovery."
    },
    {
      "start": 100.48,
      "end": 106.24,
      "text": "The supposed research institute backing the study was nothing more than a fabricated website."
    },
    {
      "start": 107.14,
      "end": 117.2,
      "text": "The true author was a journalist. He orchestrated the entire stunt to prove that bad science wrapped in a highly appealing headline will spread globally before anyone verifies the facts."
    },
    {
      "start": 118.04,
      "end": 125.88,
      "text": "Critical thinking requires the ability to read between those lines, particularly when our desires make us vulnerable to confirmation bias."
    },
    {
      "start": 125.88,
      "end": 130.2,
      "text": "This board outlines five habits you can build to sharpen your thinking."
    },
    {
      "start": 130.98,
      "end": 133.22,
      "text": "Every single one functions as a direct question."
    },
    {
      "start": 133.98,
      "end": 135.92,
      "text": "Habit one. Is it actually right?"
    },
    {
      "start": 136.58,
      "end": 141.5,
      "text": "You check what evidence supports the claim and verify if that evidence genuinely aligns with the conclusion."
    },
    {
      "start": 142.3,
      "end": 144.32,
      "text": "Habit two. Do I know enough to judge?"
    },
    {
      "start": 144.98,
      "end": 149.98,
      "text": "You have to recognize the limits of your own knowledge and identify exactly what you still need to learn."
    },
    {
      "start": 149.98,
      "end": 156.18,
      "text": "Habit three. What's missing? You actively hunt for omitted information or alternative explanations."
    },
    {
      "start": 157.2,
      "end": 158.94,
      "text": "Habit four. Why am I convinced?"
    },
    {
      "start": 159.62,
      "end": 166.32,
      "text": "You audit your own reaction to see if you are persuaded by hard evidence, confident freezing, or simply your own desire to believe it."
    },
    {
      "start": 166.94,
      "end": 168.52,
      "text": "Habit five. What's my call?"
    },
    {
      "start": 169.16,
      "end": 174.02,
      "text": "You make a decision on what to believe while maintaining the flexibility to change your mind when new data arrives."
    },
    {
      "start": 174.02,
      "end": 181.24,
      "text": "These five questions work on anything you read or hear, and they are especially vital when working with artificial intelligence."
    },
    {
      "start": 182.0,
      "end": 186.16,
      "text": "AI can help you find a flaw in your thinking or confidently repeat it."
    },
    {
      "start": 186.76,
      "end": 188.32,
      "text": "You need to judge which is happening."
    },
    {
      "start": 189.1,
      "end": 191.08,
      "text": "AI amplifies whatever you bring to it."
    },
    {
      "start": 191.68,
      "end": 193.28,
      "text": "Good thinking in, sharper output."
    }
  ],
